{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] explicitly states that transaction IDs have a limited size of 32 bits.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "But since transaction IDs have limited size (32 bits)..."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] mentions that a cluster running for more than 4 billion transactions would suffer wraparound, implying the space holds about 4 billion values. Passage [1] confirms the modulo-2^32 arithmetic, which corresponds to approximately 4.29 billion values.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "...a cluster that runs for a long time (more than 4 billion transactions) would suffer transaction ID wraparound..."
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "Normal XIDs are compared using modulo-232 arithmetic."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [1] explicitly states that due to circular comparison, for every normal XID, there are two billion older and two billion newer XIDs.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "This means that for every normal XID, there are two billion XIDs that are “older” and two billion that are “newer”..."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] states that to avoid catastrophic data loss from wraparound, it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "To avoid this, it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] describes the result as 'catastrophic data loss' but immediately clarifies in parentheses that the data is still there, just inaccessible ('cold comfort if you cannot get at it'). This contradicts the claim that the loss is 'permanent, unrecoverable'. Furthermore, passages [7] and [8] describe steps to restore normal operation, implying recoverability.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "In short, catastrophic data loss. (Actually the data is still there, but that's cold comfort if you cannot get at it.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c6",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages do not provide information linking the default `autovacuum_freeze_max_age` value to a percentage of the two-billion window in the manner described. While Passage [5] gives the default value (200 million) and Passage [0]/[1] give the total window (~2 billion or ~4 billion depending on interpretation of 'window'), the specific calculation of 'about 10%' is an inference not explicitly stated or supported by the text provided.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c7",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [4] states the effective maximum for `vacuum_freeze_table_age` is 0.95 * `autovacuum_freeze_max_age`. Passage [5] states the default `autovacuum_freeze_max_age` is 200 million. 0.95 * 200 million = 190 million. The claim says 'at most 180 million', which is incorrect based on the math (190 > 180). Wait, let me re-read carefully. The claim says 'can be at most 180 million'. If the max is 190 million, then it can be 190 million. Therefore, saying it can be *at most* 180 million is false because it can be higher (up to 190m). Thus, the claim is CONTRADICTED by the calculation derived from the text.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "The effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 0.95 * autovacuum_freeze_max_age..."
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "(The default, 200 million transactions...)"
        }
      ],
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c8",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [6] states warnings begin when oldest XIDs reach forty million transactions from the wraparound point. Passage [7] states the system refuses new XIDs when fewer than three million transactions are left until wraparound. The difference between 40 million and 3 million is 37 million.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "...when the database's oldest XIDs reach forty million transactions from the wraparound point:"
        },
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "...the system will refuse to assign new XIDs once there are fewer than three million transactions left until wraparound:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c9",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [8] explicitly states that in this condition, 'only read-only transactions can be started'. It does not say *no* new transactions of any kind can be started; read-only ones are allowed.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 8,
          "quote": "In this condition any transactions already in progress can continue, but only read-only transactions can be started."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

