{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] explicitly states that all processes using a database must be on the same host computer because WAL requires shared memory, which cannot be shared across separate host machines.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "All processes using a database must be on the same host computer; WAL does not work over a network filesystem."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] and Passage [1] state that WAL does not work over a network filesystem (like NFS) because it requires shared memory, which processes on different hosts cannot share. Therefore, two processes on different machines sharing a database over NFS cannot use WAL in standard modes.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "WAL does not work over a network filesystem. This is because WAL requires all processes to share a small amount of memory and processes on separate host machines obviously cannot share memory with each other."
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "This is why the write-ahead log implementation will not work on a network filesystem."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages state that being on the same host is *necessary* for WAL to work (due to shared memory requirements), but they do not claim it is *sufficient* or enough to guarantee it works. Other factors like VFS support for shared memory methods are also required (see Passage [5]).",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] explicitly states that since there is only one WAL file, there can only be one writer at a time.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "However, since there is only one WAL file, there can only be one writer at a time."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] states that writers and readers can run at the same time because writers do nothing that would interfere with the actions of readers.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "Because writers do nothing that would interfere with the actions of readers, writers and readers can run at the same time."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c6",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The provided passages do not mention 'BEGIN CONCURRENT' or any mechanism to lift the single-writer limit. Passage [2] asserts there can only be one writer at a time.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c7",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [3] states that WAL databases can be created/read/written without shared memory as long as locking_mode is set to EXCLUSIVE before the first attempted access.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "WAL databases can be created, read, and written even if shared memory is unavailable as long as the locking_mode is set to EXCLUSIVE before the first attempted access."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c8",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [4] explains that if EXCLUSIVE mode is set prior to the first access, no shared-memory wal-index is created, and the connection remains in EXCLUSIVE mode as long as the journal mode is WAL. It further states the only way to change out of EXCLUSIVE locking mode is to first change out of WAL journal mode.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "In that case, the database connection remains in EXCLUSIVE mode as long as the journal mode is WAL... The only way to change out of EXCLUSIVE locking mode is to first change out of WAL journal mode."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c9",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [6] states that writers omit the sync if PRAGMA synchronous is set to NORMAL.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "(Writers sync the WAL on every transaction commit if PRAGMA synchronous is set to FULL but omit this sync if PRAGMA synchronous is set to NORMAL.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c10",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [7] explicitly notes that with PRAGMA synchronous set to NORMAL, the checkpoint is the only operation to issue an I/O barrier or sync operation.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "Note that with PRAGMA synchronous set to NORMAL, the checkpoint is the only operation to issue an I/O barrier or sync operation..."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c11",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [6] states that writers sync the WAL on every transaction commit if PRAGMA synchronous is set to FULL.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "Writers sync the WAL on every transaction commit if PRAGMA synchronous is set to FULL..."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c12",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [7] specifies that running the checkpoint in a separate thread prevents blocking on sync operations specifically when PRAGMA synchronous is set to NORMAL. If synchronous is set to FULL, commits themselves perform syncs (as per Passage [6]), so the main thread doing updates would still block on syncs during commits, regardless of where the checkpoint runs.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "Note that with PRAGMA synchronous set to NORMAL... If an application therefore runs checkpoint in a separate thread or process, the main thread or process that is doing database queries and updates will never block on a sync operation."
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "Writers sync the WAL on every transaction commit if PRAGMA synchronous is set to FULL..."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

